"Inside a Quasar",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (quasars) and §{#} (supermassive black holes: what quasars really are). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> three views, chosen above the picture. Flicker means small: Brightens in an hour, Changes over a week, A light year across. The engine: One Sun of gas a year, Fusion instead, Too much light: the Eddington limit, A small black hole glows in X-rays. Jets faster than light?: M87’s jet: faster than light?, A jet seen from the side. This worksheet uses all three views.",
"Flicker means small shows a glowing ball seen from far to the right. Every part of it brightens at the same instant, but light from the far side has farther to travel, so we see the change spread out over the time light takes to cross the ball.",
"The engine shows a black hole of mass M fed \\(\\dot m\\) solar masses of gas a year, with its hot disk. The share of the mass turned into light, η, is chosen with the buttons under the sliders. The readouts give the power L, the Eddington limit and their ratio.",
"Jets faster than light? shows a blob shot out of the core at speed v at an angle θ to our line of sight, with a dot for the light it gives off each year, and a graph of what a telescope sees.",
"To set a value exactly, click the readout beside a slider, type the value and press Enter (for example {#} for {#} solar masses a year, or {#} for \\(3\\times10^8\\) solar masses).",
"Record reading stores the engine’s mass, feeding rate, η, power, Eddington limit and ratio in The engine view, or the blob’s speed, angle, the time between arrivals, the distance across the sky and the apparent speed in the jet view. Export to worksheet sends engine readings to the first Data table and jet readings to the third. Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of its table.",
"Find out how big a quasar can be from how fast it flickers, how much power a black hole makes from the gas it swallows, and why blobs in quasar jets can seem to cross the sky faster than light.",
"<strong>Flicker means small.</strong> A quasar is a point of light that outshines a whole galaxy of a hundred billion stars, and many quasars change brightness in days or even hours. Suppose every part of a glowing ball brightened at the same instant. Light from the near side would reach us first, and light from the far side a little later, because it has to cross the ball. A change we see happening in a time t must come from a region no bigger than the distance light goes in that time,",
"\\[D \\approx c\\,t,\\]",
"with \\(c = 3.00\\times10^5\\) km/s. One AU is \\(1.496\\times10^8\\) km, and one light year is {#} AU.",
"<strong>The engine.</strong> Gas falling toward a black hole swirls into a flat disk, rubs, heats to over {#} K and glows. Falling in can turn about {#}% of the gas’s mass into energy; hydrogen fusion in stars turns only {#}%. If η is that share and \\(\\dot m\\) is the mass swallowed each second, the power is",
"\\[L = \\eta\\,\\dot m\\,c^2,\\]",
"with \\(c = 3.00\\times10^8\\) m/s, the mass in kg and L in watts. One solar mass is \\(1.988\\times10^{30}\\) kg, one year is \\(3.156\\times10^7\\) s, and the Sun gives out \\(L_\\odot = 3.83\\times10^{26}\\) W.",
"<strong>Too much light.</strong> Light pushes on the infalling gas. At the Eddington limit the push balances the black hole’s pull; a brighter source would blow its own fuel away. The limit is proportional to the black hole’s mass: \\(L_{\\text{Edd}} = 1.26\\times10^{31}\\) W for every solar mass. So a quasar that shines steadily must hold a black hole of at least \\(L/(1.26\\times10^{31}\\ \\text{W})\\) solar masses.",
"<strong>Jets.</strong> Some quasars shoot out jets of gas at nearly the speed of light. A blob moving nearly toward us almost keeps up with its own light: light it gives off a year apart reaches us much less than a year apart. Its apparent speed across the sky is the distance it moves sideways in one year, in light years, divided by the time between the arrivals of that light, in years.",
"A quasar brightens noticeably in one hour. How big can it be, in km and in AU?",
"A black hole swallows one solar mass of gas a year and turns {#}% of it into energy. Find the power in watts and in Suns.",
"A blob in a quasar’s jet moves at {#} times the speed of light, aimed {#}° from our line of sight, almost straight at us. Measured with a telescope over several years, will it seem to cross the sky slower than light, at about the speed of light, or faster than light? Commit to one and say why before you open the demo.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Flicker means small.</strong> The page opens in <strong>Flicker means small</strong> with the setup <strong>Brightens in an hour</strong>. In the second Data table, write the <strong>Change takes t</strong>, the <strong>Biggest size D = c t</strong> in km and the <strong>in AU</strong> readout. Press <strong>Changes over a week</strong> and <strong>A light year across</strong> and fill a row for each.",
"<strong>Part {#}. The engine.</strong> Press <strong>The engine</strong> above the picture, then the setup <strong>One Sun of gas a year</strong> (M = \\(10^8\\) M☉, Typical {#}%). Set <strong>Gas swallowed ṁ</strong> to {#} M☉/yr (click its readout and type {#}) and press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Record again at {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} M☉/yr. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>, then <strong>Clear</strong>.",
"<strong>Part {#}. The limit and fusion.</strong> Keep ṁ at {#} M☉/yr. Set <strong>Black hole mass M</strong> to \\(3\\times10^8\\) (type {#}) and record. Set M back to {#} and ṁ to {#}, press <strong>Fusion {#}%</strong> under Share of the mass turned into energy, and record. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: these two go into a new copy of the first table.",
"<strong>Part {#}. A jet.</strong> Press <strong>Jets faster than light?</strong> above the picture, then <strong>M87’s jet: faster than light?</strong> ({#} c at {#}°). Press <strong>Play</strong> and watch the dots of light reach the telescope. Press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Compare the <strong>Apparent speed across the sky</strong> with your guess in Before the lab {#}. Then set <strong>Angle from our line of sight θ</strong> to {#}°, {#}° and {#}° (click its readout to type) and record each. Press <strong>Angle for the biggest apparent speed</strong> and record. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: the five readings fill the third Data table.",
